/* Exam 3 "Hear the why": spoken explanations for q_L17.js and q_WHY_L17.js. Loaded after narration3.js. */
Object.assign(NARR_Q, {
"L17-001": "Before genetics could explain it, inter-individual variation in drug response was already associated with four factors: age, ethnicity, gender and underlying disease. Pharmacogenomics exists because people respond very differently to the same drug. One person can have a lethal side effect while another is not affected at all. In class she said that these variations were associated with age and ethnicity and gender and underlying disease, and that with our progress in genetics we can now explain some of the variation by genetics. Genetic variation is the fifth factor on the list, the one that knowledge of genes added. Allele frequency does not belong on the list. It is a population measure, how common an allele is, and not a factor that was linked to drug response before genetics.",
"L17-002": "One of the first indications that people respond to a drug differently because of their genetics was hemolytic anemia with primaquine in the Korean War. Primaquine is an antimalarial used as a prophylactic, and in the nineteen fifties it was given widely to military personnel in the field. Some soldiers developed hemolytic anemia, and clinicians noticed that the bad responses fell along the lines of ethnicity: soldiers of Middle Eastern or African descent seemed most at risk. She called it the first real data point linking a bad drug response to an inherited trait. The genetic link was made in nineteen fifty six. The other events came later. Motulsky's statement that inheritance might explain differences in drug response came in nineteen fifty seven, and it was a proposal, not the first observation. The twin studies of antipyrine half-life came later still, and the digoxin and P-glycoprotein example belongs to drug transporters.",
"L17-003": "The soldiers who developed hemolytic anemia on primaquine had low activity of the enzyme glucose six phosphate dehydrogenase, called G six P D, in their red blood cells. Clinicians in the Korean War observed that soldiers of Middle Eastern or African descent seemed to be the ones responding badly. The reaction was significant methemoglobinemia leading to hemolytic anemia. When the enzyme activity was measured, those with the bad response had a deficiency in G six P D activity. The other proteins belong to later parts of the topic. Cytochrome P four fifty two C nineteen is the running example for genotype and phenotype. A low-activity P-glycoprotein is the digoxin example of a drug transporter. Variants of the human leukocyte antigens, called H L A antigens, belong to the immune system target area.",
"L17-004": "In the nineteen fifties no one could genotype a patient, but the activity of an enzyme could be measured. The soldiers who reacted badly to primaquine had low activity of glucose six phosphate dehydrogenase, G six P D, in their red blood cells. That enzyme activity is a phenotype, and enough was known to say that it reflected a genetic variation. In class she said that we couldn't genotype at that time, but we could measure that enzyme activity, and we realized that the phenotype was reflecting these people's genotype. Reading the D N A sequence came much later. Comparing half-lives in twin pairs was the later twin study design. Methemoglobinemia was part of the reaction itself, not the measurement that linked it to genetics.",
"L17-006": "Friedrich Vogel introduced the term pharmacogenetics in nineteen fifty nine. It was the first word coined to link pharmacology, or drugs, to genetics. In class she said that in nineteen fifty nine Vogel introduced the term, the first word he coined to link drugs to genetics. The other names on the timeline did other things. Watson and Crick described the structure of D N A in nineteen fifty three. Motulsky proposed in nineteen fifty seven that inheritance might explain many individual differences in drug efficacy and adverse drug reactions, but he did not coin the term. The Human Genome Project started in nineteen ninety and was completed in two thousand three.",
"L17-007": "The Human Genome Project put each sequence a lab produced into a database that everyone could access. It ran from nineteen ninety to two thousand three as a worldwide consortium of academics, industry and government agencies, with each lab sequencing a stretch of D N A. In class she said that once one lab found the sequence for one chunk of the human genome, it was put in a database and everybody had access to it, and she called that one of the most amazing parts of the project. Scientists usually keep their work private so that nobody publishes it first, and the project broke that habit. It was completed early and under budget. It was not one agency working alone. Sequencing a whole genome in hours came later, built on very much faster methods developed during the project, which began with very slow but proven methods.",
"L17-008": "Identical twin pairs have matching half-lives while fraternal twin pairs vary. Antipyrine is cleared entirely by metabolism, essentially by one enzyme, so its half-life is a clean readout of that enzyme's activity. Identical twins share the same genotype. Fraternal twins are related but do not share the same genotype, almost like random people from the population, in her words. In class she said that almost all the identical twin pairs had exactly the same clearance, and that the fraternal pairs were very variable, with only pair D a little similar. If genes set how fast the enzyme works, this is the pattern expected, so variability in drug metabolism is heritable. If the fraternal pairs had matched too, sharing a genotype would make no difference. If both kinds of pair varied, genes would not explain the variation. The reversed pattern would mean half-life does not follow genetic sameness.",
"L17-009": "Antipyrine was a good drug for the twin study because it is cleared entirely by metabolism, essentially by one enzyme. Its half-life therefore gave a clean measure of whether that enzyme was working in the normal range. The study measured the half-life of clearance, which is a phenotype. In class she said that we did not genotype these people at this time; we were looking at their phenotype, which was the half-life of the clearance of the drug. No twin was genotyped, because genotyping came later. A drug cleared by many enzymes would give an unclear phenotype. Antipyrine is not cleared by the kidney, so creatinine has nothing to do with it.",
"L17-010": "Pharmacogenetics is the term for one patient, and pharmacogenomics is the broader, genome-wide term for populations. Pharmacogenetics is the study of genetic causes of individual variations in drug response. Pharmacogenomics more broadly involves genome-wide analysis of the genetic determinants of drug efficacy and toxicity, and it also covers genomics technologies such as bioengineered proteins and gene therapy. In class she said that pharmacogenetics is the term we use for an individual, one patient, and their response to a drug, and that pharmacogenomics is a more global term, for populations as a whole. The two terms are used interchangeably, and the preferred term is pharmacogenomics. The reversed pairing is the trap. The difference is scope. It is not metabolizing enzymes against drug targets, and it is not phenotype against genotype.",
"L17-011": "The two terms are used interchangeably, and the preferred term is pharmacogenomics. Technically they differ: pharmacogenetics is about one patient, and pharmacogenomics is about populations and the whole genome. In practice even people renowned in the field use them interchangeably, and she said that most of the time she says pharmacogenomics. The definitions come from the American Association of Pharmaceutical Scientists Pharmacogenomics Focus Group. She first named the World Health Organization, then corrected herself to that focus group, and added that the World Health Organization also publishes definitions. The traps change one half of the statement. Saying the terms are not interchangeable is wrong, because experts use them interchangeably. Naming pharmacogenetics as the preferred term is wrong, because the preferred term is pharmacogenomics.",
"L17-012": "Organizations such as the American Association of Pharmaceutical Scientists and the World Health Organization publish definitions so that everyone uses a term to mean the same thing. If drug companies, regulators and researchers define genotypes or sample populations differently, the data become messy, and one country's agency cannot compare its data with another's. In class she said that the reason the world gets together and writes these definitions is that we have better scientific communication. Keeping each country's data separate is the reverse of the purpose. Which drugs need genotyping first is a clinical decision. Dropping the old word mutant for variant is a change in usage, not the reason definitions are published.",
"L17-013": "MacKinnon and colleagues, in two thousand twenty three, reviewed the medical records of thousands of patients and found that more than half were prescribed medications where pharmacogenomics is important in their use. In class she said that they did not evaluate whether pharmacogenomics was actually used as these patients were prescribed and treated. So the finding is about how many patients are on such drugs, not about whether genetics guided the prescriptions. The finding about pharmacists comes from a different study. Wondrasek and colleagues, in two thousand twenty four, found that pharmacists consider pharmacogenomics important but do not feel confident using it. That prescribers are less versed than pharmacists was her point about education.",
"L17-014": "Wondrasek and colleagues, in two thousand twenty four, asked pharmacists about their knowledge of and comfort with pharmacogenomics. The finding has two halves. Pharmacists feel pharmacogenomics is important, but they do not feel confident in their knowledge of it or in their ability to use it. In class she said that although pharmacists feel pharmacogenomics is important, they don't feel confident in their knowledge or their ability to use it. The gap exists even though pharmacy students are the best educated in pharmacogenomics. The traps change one half: pharmacists do rate it as important, and they do not feel confident. More than half of patients on drugs where pharmacogenomics matters is the MacKinnon finding from patient records.",
"L17-015": "Anybody who prescribes should know pharmacogenomics, and prescribers are less versed in it than pharmacists. Pharmacogenomics is pharmacy with genetics. Prescribers do not spend as much time with drugs as pharmacists do, so they are not yet as well versed in it. In class she said that our prescribers are not as well versed yet in pharmacogenomics, because they don't spend as much time with drugs as pharmacy students do. Pharmacy students are the best educated in pharmacogenomics. Nurses and other providers should know it too, but they are not better versed than pharmacists. Making prescribers better or equally versed reverses her point.",
"L17-016": "It is better to genotype the patient before giving the drug when the drug can quickly cause a lethal side effect. Often a patient's response prompts a look at the genotype afterward. That response may be a side effect or a lack of efficacy, and looking afterward is acceptable when the side effect is not lethal. For a drug with a possible lethal side effect, the version of the gene should be known first. In class she said that for some medications genotyping first is standard protocol, and a clinician could be liable for not doing it. A drug that has already failed, a side effect that has already happened, and a mild possible side effect are all situations where genotyping afterward is acceptable.",
"L17-017": "The four practice areas named for pharmacogenomics are oncology, the central nervous system, cardiology and infectious disease. In class she said that in oncology you will be using pharmacogenomics all the time, that central nervous system pharmacogenomics is big, that some cardiology pharmacogenomics is big, and that infectious disease is doing more and more of it. She expects pharmacogenomics to be part of every area of pharmacy to some degree. The field moves rapidly, so pharmacists need the basics and a way of thinking that lets them keep learning after graduation. Dermatology was not one of the areas she named.",
"L17-018": "The four target areas are metabolizing enzymes, drug transporters, drug targets and immune system targets. In class she said that these are the four areas, that you do need to know these, and named metabolizing enzymes, transporters, drug targets, and then immunological targets. The printed title says three target areas; she changed it to four by adding immune system targets. The areas came in that order. Metabolizing enzymes came first, the cytochrome P four fifties and now other phase one and phase two enzymes, because variants that blocked clearance drove blood levels too high and sometimes killed patients. Transporters followed, with P-glycoprotein and digoxin as the example. Drug targets are receptors or enzymes a drug acts on, and their biggest role now is in oncology. Immune system targets are the newest area, with variants of the human leukocyte antigens, called H L A antigens, tied to severe adverse drug reactions. Plasma protein binding is not a target area. It is part of distribution, one step of pharmacokinetics.",
"L17-019": "We know the most about variants in metabolizing enzymes because their variants caused the most apparent and most dangerous reactions. She called them the low hanging fruit, meaning the problems that were easiest to find and explain first. Some patients could not metabolize and clear certain drugs, their blood concentrations went too high, and sometimes patients died. The cytochrome P four fifties were studied first, and now other phase one and phase two enzymes are too. Variants in these enzymes cause differences in drug plasma concentrations between people, which may have serious implications for narrow therapeutic index drugs. Metabolizing enzymes are therefore the best characterized group of variants that affect drug response, ahead of transporters and drug targets. Reduced efficacy is the outcome of drug-target variants. The twin studies measured only a phenotype, and the under one percent cutoff defines a rare variant, which has nothing to do with why this area came first.",
"L17-020": "P-glycoprotein is a drug transporter expressed in the gut. It is encoded by the A B C B one gene, and it is an efflux transporter. Many genetic variants have been defined in that gene. As an oral drug tries to cross the cells lining the gut lumen, P-glycoprotein grabs it and pumps it back into the lumen, to be passed in the stool. In class she said that P-glycoprotein is encoded by the A B C B one gene, and that it is an efflux transporter. It is not an uptake transporter. Other transporters are expressed in the liver, the brain and elsewhere, and their variants are an active research area. Cytochrome P four fifty two C nineteen is the gene for a metabolizing enzyme, not for a transporter. Glucose six phosphate dehydrogenase is the red blood cell enzyme from the primaquine history, not a transporter.",
"L17-021": "Being a substrate for P-glycoprotein in the gut affects an oral drug's bioavailability. P-glycoprotein is an efflux transporter. As the drug tries to cross the gut lumen cells, P-glycoprotein pumps it back into the lumen, so less of the dose is absorbed into the systemic circulation. How much of an oral dose reaches the systemic circulation is bioavailability. In class she said she was thinking of absorption into the systemic circulation, and that she thinks about bioavailability a lot with oral drugs that are substrates for P-glycoprotein. Excretion was offered as an answer, but gut P-glycoprotein sends the drug out in the stool, not the urine. Distribution comes after absorption, and hepatic metabolism is the work of metabolizing enzymes.",
"L17-022": "With a low-activity P-glycoprotein, digoxin's bioavailability goes up. Digoxin is a substrate for P-glycoprotein in the gut, and its dose is set knowing that P-glycoprotein blocks part of its absorption. A low-activity version pumps less digoxin back into the lumen, so more of the dose reaches the systemic circulation. Digoxin has a very narrow therapeutic index, so blood levels can become high on a normal dose and affect heart rate too much. In class she asked whether the bioavailability goes up or down, and answered that it goes up. It does not go down, because less efflux means more absorption. It is not unchanged or unpredictable, because the change follows directly from what P-glycoprotein does.",
"L17-023": "Genetic variation in drug targets has its biggest role right now in oncology. A drug target is where a drug acts, such as a receptor or an enzyme the drug inhibits. Many cancer drugs, and some immunotherapy drugs, are designed to hit a specific protein that the cancer cell expresses, so whether the patient's cancer has that target decides whether the drug works at all. In class she said that the area where drug targets have the biggest role right now is oncology. The drug target area has moved more slowly than metabolizing enzymes or transporters. Cardiology has an evidence-based guidance document on variants of beta adrenergic receptors, published two years ago, but it is not the biggest area. Infectious disease and central nervous system practice use pharmacogenomics more and more, but oncology is the area she named for drug targets.",
"L17-024": "The newest target area of pharmacogenomics is the immune system, and it involves variants of the human leukocyte antigens, called H L A antigens. In class she said that people with certain variants of their H L A antigens are going to respond badly to certain drugs, and she tied these variants to severe adverse drug reactions. Every year the list of drugs and related H L A variants in the literature gets a little longer. The other areas came earlier. Metabolizing enzymes such as the cytochrome P four fifties were the first and best known area. Drug transporters, with the A B C B one gene that encodes P-glycoprotein, were the second. Drug targets, such as beta adrenergic receptors, were the third. The order is metabolizing enzymes, transporters, drug targets, then the immune system.",
"L17-027": "If the patient's cancer does not express the protein the drug binds, the drug should not be given. Targeted cancer drugs are designed to hit a specific protein that the cancer cell expresses. Without that protein the drug has nothing to bind, so it will not work. It is expensive, and it can still cause side effects. In class she said that if their cancer doesn't have that protein, don't give them this drug, because it's expensive and it can cause side effects. This belongs to the efficacy bucket. A higher dose cannot make the drug work without its target. Giving it and watching for toxicity still exposes the patient to side effects with no benefit. Genotyping liver enzymes is a pharmacokinetic question, and here the drug target is missing.",
"L17-028": "The wild type is usually the most common version of a gene, and a variant is a version that is not the most common. In class she said that wild type is used for the version of a gene that is the most common, usually, and that she accepts most common as the answer. The word usually matters because in the genetics literature the version called wild type is sometimes simply the one scientists understood first. The word mutant is no longer used for a variant. The reversed pairing is the trap. The one percent cutoff separates a polymorphism from a rare variant, not wild type from variant. The copies from the mother and the father are a person's two alleles.",
"L17-029": "What separates a polymorphism from a rare variant is frequency. A polymorphism is a genetic variant present in at least one percent of a population. A rare variant is present in less than one percent. In class she said that by definition a polymorphism occurs at least one percent of the time in a population, and that a rare variant is a genetic variation occurring in less than one percent. The cutoff is one percent, not ten percent. The kind of change does not enter the definition, so a one-nucleotide change and a whole gene deletion can both be polymorphisms. How harmful the variant is does not enter it either. A rare variant can be just as dangerous for the patient who has it.",
"L17-030": "A whole gene deletion found in more than one percent of a population is a polymorphism. Many people think polymorphisms are only single nucleotide changes, but that is not true. The definition speaks only to how often a change occurs. In class she said that any kind of genetic change, if it happens in one percent or more of the population, is by definition polymorphic, because the polymorphism definition speaks to the frequency of that change. So a whole gene deletion, extra copies of a gene or a sister chromatid exchange can each be a polymorphism. A rare variant is the same kind of change in less than one percent. A single nucleotide polymorphism, called a snip, changes only one nucleotide, and a whole gene deletion is not that. A haplotype is a set of alleles at several loci on one chromosome.",
"L17-031": "The term rare variant entered pharmacogenomics because some patients reacted badly without carrying the known polymorphism. Polymorphisms, present in at least one percent of a population, were characterized first and explain much of the variation in drug response. A closer look at the genes of these patients showed a variation present in less than one percent of people. In class she said that this is a person with a rare variant making them respond badly, and that the term is a more recent addition as understanding progressed. Polymorphisms did not turn out to have little effect. Ease of testing is not where the term came from. Mutant is an older word for variant, while rare variant is a frequency term.",
"L17-032": "A single nucleotide polymorphism, called a snip, means that just one nucleotide in a gene differs from the wild type sequence. In class she said that just one nucleotide is different in the gene sequence for your patient with a polymorphism compared to your patient with the wild type version. It is the most common type of polymorphism. Like every polymorphism, it occurs in at least one percent of a population. The same one-nucleotide change in less than one percent of people would be a rare variant, not a polymorphism. A whole gene deletion is a different kind of polymorphism. Alleles at several loci that co-exist on one chromosome describe a haplotype.",
"L17-033": "A single nucleotide change, an entire gene deletion and extra copies of a gene can all be polymorphisms. The label depends only on how often a variation in the D N A sequence occurs: in at least one percent of a population. In class she said that other kinds of genetic variation are also called polymorphisms, including more than one nucleotide change, an entire gene insertion or deletion, or extra copies of a gene, and that most people think polymorphisms are just snips, which is not true. D N A methylation without a sequence change is not a polymorphism. It is an epigenetic factor that affects how genes are expressed. A change in expression from diet or stress is an environmental effect, and it does not change the D N A sequence either.",
"L17-034": "An allele is one of the two copies of a gene that a person carries. The definition from the National Human Genome Research Institute is the variant and wild type forms of a gene at a particular location on a chromosome. Humans are diploid, so we generally have two copies of every chromosome and two copies of each gene. One copy comes from the biological mother and one from the biological father. Each nucleotide base in the gene can be considered an allele. In class she said that most of us have two copies of every gene, so one copy is an allele and the other copy is an allele. The traps are the neighboring terms. The two alleles a person has for one gene, taken together, are the genotype. A set of alleles at several loci on one chromosome is a haplotype. Any observable characteristic is a phenotype.",
"L17-035": "At the third locus the person is heterozygous for B. Compare the two alleles at each locus. At the third locus one chromosome carries big B and the other carries little b, so the two alleles differ, and that is heterozygous. Homozygous means both copies are the same, as at the first locus, with big P on both, and at the second, with little a on both. In class she said they got big B on one allele and little b on the other, so they are heterozygous, and the professional way to say it is heterozygous for B at the third locus. Homozygous for big B is wrong because only one copy is big B, and homozygous for little b is wrong because only one copy is little b. Homozygous for a describes the second locus. The figure also shows dominant and recessive letters, but pharmacogenomics does not discuss variants as dominant or recessive. Her example was eye color: blue as recessive and brown as dominant is not that clean.",
"L17-036": "A genotype is the two alleles a person has for a gene, and a phenotype is any observable characteristic. A phenotype can be morphological, developmental, biochemical, physical or behavioral, and it results from the expression of genes. In class she said that the two alleles anybody has represent their genotype, and that phenotypes are driven by the genotype. In pharmacogenomics a phenotype is often an enzyme's activity. A person with two copies of the star two allele of cytochrome P four fifty two C nineteen has no activity of that enzyme, and that is the phenotype. The traps swap the two terms, make the genotype a single allele, or make the phenotype the protein one allele makes. The protein is the gene product.",
"L17-037": "The genotype of a person homozygous for the star two allele of cytochrome P four fifty two C nineteen is written C Y P two C nineteen, star two, slash, star two. The written genotype gives the gene, then the allele on each of the two copies. In class she said that she had given the gene, told what allele they have, and that both allele copies are the same, so they are homozygous for star two. Cytochrome P four fifty two C nineteen does a lot of drug metabolism and has many genetic variations. The gene name with a single star two names the allele, not the person's two copies. Star one with star two pairs two different alleles, so that person is heterozygous. Star one on both copies is a person homozygous for star one, not for star two.",
"L17-038": "A person whose genotype is cytochrome P four fifty two C nineteen star two, star two has no activity of that enzyme. The star two variant has no enzyme activity, and this person has it on both copies, so there is no working copy left. That absence of activity is the phenotype, and the person cannot metabolize drugs through that enzyme. In class she said that the star two variant has no activity, that this is the phenotype, and that knowledge of the genotype-phenotype relationship for star two is really tight. Normal activity belongs to the wild type. Partial activity would need one working copy, and this person has none. Extra activity would come from extra working copies of the gene.",
"L17-039": "The functional significance of a genetic variation is how the change affects the function of the protein, enzyme or transporter that the gene makes. She called it a big key word in the world of pharmacogenomics. In class she said that if we don't know how that genetic variation is changing how the gene product works, we cannot explain the drug response phenotype. Variants whose functional significance is known, such as the star two allele of cytochrome P four fifty two C nineteen, can be used to decide whether a patient gets a drug or a lower dose. The traps are other properties of a variant. How often it occurs is its frequency. Where it sits on the chromosome is its locus. Several changes on one chromosome describe a haplotype.",
"L17-040": "Three things complicate the genotype-phenotype relationship. First, many polymorphisms may be present in a single gene, so it is hard to tell which variation causes the phenotype. Second, most traits are multigenic, not monogenic, and drug responses can be multigenic too: one gene's variant may have a large effect and another's a small one. Third, environmental factors influence how genes are expressed. These include diet, habits, sleep and stress, and the epigenetic factors, D N A methylation and histone modification. The statement that most traits are monogenic reverses the second complication. The statement that phenotype results from the expression of genes is the definition of phenotype, not a complication.",
"L17-041": "Many polymorphisms in one gene complicate the genotype-phenotype relationship because it is hard to tell which variation causes the phenotype. A single gene can have several places that vary, such as a single nucleotide polymorphism, or snip, in one place and a tandem repeat in another. The variations can be identified, but it takes a lot of work to find which one is responsible for the way the patient responds, and sometimes it depends on the structure of the gene. In class she asked why this complicates it, and answered that it's hard to figure out which genetic variation is causing the phenotype. Sequencing the gene is not the hard part; assigning the effect is. Environmental factors are a separate complication, and they change gene expression, not the D N A sequence. The gene still has a wild type version.",
"L17-042": "Sickle cell disease, Huntington's chorea and Tay-Sachs disease are each caused by one genetic variation. Huntington's chorea is a very specific, early-onset dementia that is very inheritable, caused by one genetic variant in one allele. Certain kinds of early-onset Alzheimer's disease and Parkinson's disease are also in this group, and Tay-Sachs disease is a metabolic disorder. These single-gene diseases are fairly rare. Most diseases are caused by multiple changes in our genetics. In class she named cardiovascular disease, diabetes and hyperlipidemia as examples, so diabetes and hyperlipidemia are multigenic and do not belong. The same idea applies to drug response. Some responses follow one variant, but many involve more than one gene, which is one of the complications of the genotype-phenotype relationship.",
"L17-043": "As the science gets more sophisticated, each gene's impact is weighted and the effects are added together. Drug response is often multigenic. A variant in one gene may have a large impact on the response, and a variant in another gene a smaller one. In class she said that we look at them together and say that this genotype and this genotype added together result in an adverse response. Using only the gene with the largest effect leaves out the others. Treating each gene as if it were monogenic is what a multigenic response rules out. Measuring a phenotype instead of the genotype is how the early twin studies worked, before genotyping was possible.",
"L17-044": "The internal factors called epigenetic factors are D N A methylation and histone modification. Histones are the proteins that D N A is wrapped on. These factors change how genes are expressed without changing the D N A sequence. In class she said that diet, habits, lack of exercise, sleep and stress could all affect how genes get expressed, and that some more internal things, the histones and the methylation of our D N A, are called epigenetic factors. Diet, exercise, sleep and stress are environmental factors, not the ones called epigenetic. Single nucleotide changes and gene deletions are changes in the sequence itself. Allele and genotype frequencies are population measures.",
"L17-045": "A haplotype is a set of alleles at multiple loci that co-exist on the same chromosome. It is more than one genetic variant grouped together, and the group co-exists at a higher frequency than would occur by chance. In class she said that a haplotype is a set of alleles at multiple loci that coexist in the same chromosome, and that it occurs at a frequency greater than random chance. In her illustration, three loci change together in three patterns, A G T, G T A and A G A. The other choices are neighboring terms. The two alleles of one gene that a person carries are a genotype. A single nucleotide change at one locus is a single nucleotide polymorphism, or snip, and a haplotype needs multiple loci. A variant present in less than one percent of a population is a rare variant.",
"L17-046": "The greater-than-chance co-occurrence of a haplotype's alleles is called linkage disequilibrium. A haplotype is a set of alleles at multiple loci on one chromosome. In class she said that a haplotype has a statistical significance, because it occurs at a frequency greater than random chance. The name for that higher frequency is linkage disequilibrium: the alleles sit on one chromosome and are inherited together. She added that the chemistry of D N A affects how likely genetic variations are to occur. Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium is the trap with the similar name, but it means that allele and genotype frequencies stay constant from generation to generation. Genotype frequency is how often a genotype occurs in a population. Heterozygosity means two different alleles at one locus.",
"L17-047": "The illustration identifies three haplotypes: A G T, G T A and A G A. Each of the three loci carries a single nucleotide polymorphism, or snip. The first locus has A or G, the second has G or T, and the third has A or T. Reading across each person, the orange person has A G T, the dark blue person has G T A, and the light blue person has A G A. In class she said that those three patterns of the loci changing happen more often than random chance, and that would be a haplotype. No sequence has the same base at all three loci, so triple letters such as A A A are wrong. Pairing the two letters possible at each locus gives the alternative snips, not the haplotypes. A rearranged set of letters puts a base at a locus where that base never appears, so read each locus in order.",
"L17-048": "Allele frequency is the relative frequency of one allele in a population, and genotype frequency is the relative frequency of one genotype. In class she said that how often a version of an allele happens in the population is the allele frequency. Because alleles make up a genotype, people are described by a genotype frequency as well. The wild type allele and the variant allele each have an allele frequency, and together they give the genotype frequency. Both are usually expressed as a proportion or a percentage. Neither is measured in one person or one family; both describe a population. Wild type against variant is not the difference either, since both kinds of allele have frequencies and both enter genotypes.",
"L17-049": "Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium means that allele and genotype frequencies in a population stay constant from generation to generation unless specific influences are introduced. In class she called it a very special kind of statistical analysis, which asks whether a genetic variation seen in a population is occurring because of evolutionary pressure or not. A study population in equilibrium is a good sample, and its results can be generalized. A population out of equilibrium has been changed by some pressure, so its results cannot be applied to everyone. The trap with the similar name is linkage disequilibrium, alleles at several loci occurring together more often than chance. At least one percent is the polymorphism cutoff. Equilibrium does not require wild type and variant alleles to occur equally often; it only requires the frequencies to stay constant across generations.",
"L17-050": "If one class responds to a drug differently from the school's past graduates because something pressured it, the class is not in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium and its data are not generalizable. Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium asks whether a variation seen in a sample is there because of a pressure, which she called evolutionary pressure. A pressured sample has changed compared with the general population, so new subjects without that pressure would have to be found. In class she said that we can't use this data because we are not in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, since something has pressured the class to respond differently. The stress in her example was hypothetical. A pressured class is not in equilibrium, so calling it in equilibrium and generalizable is wrong. Out of equilibrium never means generalizable. Linkage disequilibrium is a different idea, about alleles at several loci occurring together, and says nothing about the sample.",
"L17-051": "Authors of pharmacogenomic studies state that their population was in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium to show that the sample is good and the results are generalizable. Every pharmacogenomic study does the Hardy-Weinberg calculation. In class she said that authors always write that their study population was in equilibrium, that they pulled a great sample, and that it is generalizable. Without equilibrium the results probably cannot be extended to the world, and the paper would probably not be published. Equilibrium describes the population's allele and genotype frequencies. It does not show that each subject carried the variant or that the drug worked. Linkage disequilibrium is a separate idea, about alleles at several loci occurring together.",
"L17-101": "Pharmacogenomics came into medicine because responses to the same drug varied widely between people. In class she said the reason we have it is that we started observing a sometimes large inter-individual variation in drug therapy response, where one person could have a lethal response to the side effects of a drug and somebody else is not affected at all. Age, ethnicity, gender and underlying disease explained part of that variation. As knowledge of genes and of how genes interact with medications grew, genetic variation explained more of it. Patients responding alike is the reverse of what was observed. Whole genome sequencing in hours came much later. More than half of patients taking drugs where pharmacogenomics matters is a recent measure of impact, not the reason the field began.",
"L17-102": "What first suggested an inherited cause was that the bad responses to primaquine fell along the lines of ethnicity. Everybody was given primaquine as an antimalarial prophylactic, but only some soldiers developed hemolytic anemia. In class she said that the clinicians in that Korean War setting were astute enough to observe that people of Middle Eastern or African descent seemed to be the ones having the bad response. A pattern by ethnicity points to an inherited trait. Low activity of glucose six phosphate dehydrogenase in red blood cells was then found to be the phenotype reflecting the genotype. No one could genotype at that time. The pattern was ethnicity, not dose, and not every soldier developed anemia.",
"L17-103": "The twin studies compared identical with fraternal pairs to show that variability in drug metabolism is heritable. Identical twins share a genotype. Fraternal twins are related, but their genotypes differ, almost like random people from the population, in her words. Each twin got the same drug, and the half-life was compared within each pair. Almost all identical pairs had the same half-life, while the fraternal pairs varied, so genes explain the difference. In class she said that they really wanted to show heritability, so they used identical twins and then fraternal twins. No one was genotyped. The measure was clearance by metabolism, not absorption, and the design tested genes, not diet or habits.",
"L17-104": "The twin studies measured antipyrine half-life instead of genotype because the ability to genotype people came later. The half-life of clearance was a phenotype that could be measured at the time. Antipyrine is cleared entirely by metabolism by one enzyme, so its half-life showed cleanly whether that enzyme was working in the normal range. In class she said that we did not genotype these people at this time, and that as we got the ability to genotype people, we got more sophisticated in evaluating drug response based on genetics. Half-life is only one of many possible phenotypes. Antipyrine is cleared by an enzyme, not without one. Knowing that identical twins share a genotype does not tell anyone what that genotype is.",
"L17-105": "Whole genome sequencing can now be done in hours because of methods developed during the Human Genome Project. The project began in nineteen ninety with very slow but proven methods. While it ran, people worked on how to sequence faster and prepared new protocols, procedures and machines. In class she said that when the project was done, a whole lot of technological advances had occurred that let us do D N A sequencing a lot faster, so we can now do whole genome sequencing in a matter of hours. The project itself did not sequence genomes in hours. The regions between coding D N A have now been sequenced as well, not skipped. Twin studies measured a phenotype and did not replace sequencing.",
"L17-106": "The labs shared each sequence because the people who planned the project saw the benefit to the world of sharing the data. Scientists usually hide their work so that nobody publishes it first. In class she said that the people who dreamed up this project set it up so that everybody was giving and altruistic and willing to share the data, because they realized the benefit to the world. The project was a worldwide consortium of academics, industry and government agencies, so no single lab published the whole genome. Industry was one partner, and the sharing was described as altruistic, not as a contract requirement. Wanting to publish first is the usual habit the project broke.",
"L17-107": "Pharmacists will need to keep educating themselves because the field keeps moving forward rapidly. The gain and sharing of knowledge and the technology developed during the Human Genome Project sped the field up, and more information is added all the time. So the course sets up basic examples, a basic understanding and a way of thinking that pharmacists can keep using in practice. Pharmacogenomics is expected in every area of pharmacy to some degree. Oncology uses it all the time, it is big in central nervous system and cardiology practice, and infectious disease uses more and more of it. The basic terms are not what keeps changing. Pharmacy school does teach pharmacogenomics, and pharmacy students are the best educated in it. Prescribers are not better versed than pharmacists; it is the other way around.",
"L17-108": "Prescribers are not yet as well versed in pharmacogenomics because they spend less time with drugs than pharmacists do. Pharmacy students are the best taught in it because it is pharmacy with genetics. In class she said that our prescribers are not as well versed yet in pharmacogenomics, because they don't spend as much time with drugs. Anybody prescribing should still know it, and so should nurses and other providers. Prescribers do prescribe drugs affected by genes: MacKinnon and colleagues found more than half of patients were on medications where pharmacogenomics is important. Access to test results is not the gap she described, and pharmacogenomics applies to prescribing as well as dispensing.",
"L17-109": "Being a P-glycoprotein substrate affects bioavailability because P-glycoprotein pumps the drug back into the gut lumen. P-glycoprotein, encoded by the A B C B one gene, is an efflux transporter in the gut. In class she said that the drug in the gut lumen is trying to go across those lumen cells, and P-glycoprotein is grabbing it and pumping it back into the lumen, to be passed in the stool. Less of the dose then reaches the systemic circulation, and the amount absorbed into the systemic circulation is the bioavailability. P-glycoprotein is a transporter, so it does not metabolize the drug. It moves drug back into the lumen, not into the blood. Excretion by the kidney is not the effect of gut P-glycoprotein.",
"L17-110": "Digoxin's bioavailability goes up with a low-activity P-glycoprotein because less digoxin is pumped back into the gut lumen. Digoxin is a substrate for P-glycoprotein in the gut. In class she said that a person with a low-activity version is not grabbing so much drug and preventing it from being absorbed; it is letting the drug go through, because it is not doing much effluxing. More digoxin then reaches the systemic circulation. Pumping more back into the lumen would lower bioavailability. Slower metabolism in the liver belongs to the metabolizing enzyme area, not to a transporter in the gut. Kidney excretion is not what changes, because bioavailability here means absorption from the gut.",
"L17-111": "A normal digoxin dose can give high blood levels because the dose assumes that P-glycoprotein blocks part of digoxin's absorption. Digoxin is a heart-rate drug with a very narrow therapeutic index. In class she said that we dose it because we know that it is a substrate for P-glycoprotein, and that P-glycoprotein grabs the drug and stops it from being absorbed into the systemic circulation a lot of the time. A patient with low-activity P-glycoprotein absorbs more of the same dose, so blood levels rise and heart rate is affected too much. The dose was not set for low-activity patients; it assumes normal activity. P-glycoprotein does not metabolize digoxin, and the narrow therapeutic index is why a rise in absorption matters.",
"L17-112": "Drug transporters became the next area studied a lot because variants in P-glycoprotein explained high digoxin levels. Once the gene that expresses P-glycoprotein, A B C B one, was identified, variants that affect its activity were found. In class she said that this is why some patients have lots of digoxin in their bloodstream even though they got the normal dose, with heart rate affected too much, and that is why drug transporters were the next thing studied a lot. The order of the areas is metabolizing enzymes, then transporters, then drug targets, then the immune system. Metabolizing enzymes have many variants and are the best characterized. Drug targets came after transporters and moved more slowly. Reduced efficacy is the outcome of drug-target variants.",
"L17-113": "A less active metabolizing enzyme falls into the toxicity bucket because the drug is not cleared well and blood levels rise. In class she said that with that variation of the metabolizing enzyme, I may not clear the drug very well, and I could get my drug blood concentrations too high and have an adverse response or toxicity. That is a pharmacokinetic effect, and transporter variants can add to the toxicity bucket as well. A receptor that no longer responds belongs to the efficacy bucket. Less absorption from the gut would lower blood levels, not raise them. A cancer cell lacking the drug's target protein also belongs to the efficacy bucket.",
"L17-114": "A poorly responding receptor falls into the efficacy bucket because the drug's target is affected, not its blood level. A receptor is a drug target, and variants in pharmacodynamic genes change the mechanism of action and the drug response. In class she said that if a receptor has a genetic variant that makes it not respond very well, a drug trying to find that receptor and get a response cannot do it, so efficacy is affected. Build-up to toxic blood levels comes from poor clearance, which is the toxicity bucket. Being pumped back into the gut lumen is a transporter effect on absorption. Changes in absorption and excretion are pharmacokinetics, not pharmacodynamics.",
"L17-115": "The version called wild type in the literature is sometimes not the most common because it may be the version scientists understood first. Wild type usually means the most common version of a gene, and a variant is a less common version. In class she said that sometimes in the genetics literature the wild type may not be the most common, but maybe it was the one scientists understood first. She met this in her own graduate research, and it can make pulling literature on a wild type confusing, but she accepts most common as the definition. Wild type is about how common a version is, not about lack of activity; the course example of a version with no activity is the star two variant of cytochrome P four fifty two C nineteen. Less than one percent describes a rare variant. Mutant is an older word for variant, not for wild type, and it is no longer used.",
"L17-116": "A single nucleotide polymorphism and a whole gene deletion can both be polymorphisms because the term depends only on how often a change occurs. A polymorphism is a variation in D N A sequence present in at least one percent of a population. In class she said that the polymorphism definition speaks to the frequency of that change happening. So a one-nucleotide change, a change of more than one nucleotide, an entire gene insertion or deletion, or extra copies of a gene is a polymorphism whenever it occurs in at least one percent. The same change in less than one percent is a rare variant. The definition says nothing about the effect on enzyme activity, and a gene deletion is not a one-nucleotide change.",
"L17-117": "Rare variants get attention because the patient who has one still has the bad response. Each rare variant occurs in less than one percent of a population, but rarity does not matter to that patient. In class she said that it doesn't matter that their variant occurs very, very infrequently; they still feel bad when you give them that drug, or they still have a dangerous drug response. Some of these variants were found only on a closer look at a patient who responded badly without the known polymorphism. Polymorphisms, at least one percent, are the more frequent ones. Ease of genotyping is not the reason. Rare variants do not replace the wild type in most people, since by definition they are uncommon.",
"L17-118": "Each person's genotype has two alleles because humans are diploid, with one copy of each gene from each parent. We generally have two copies of every chromosome, so two copies of the same gene. One allele comes from the biological mother and one from the biological father. In class she said that we're diploid and have two copies of every gene, and that the two alleles anybody has represent their genotype. The two alleles can be the same, which is homozygous, or different, which is heterozygous, so they are not always one wild type and one variant. A gene can have many variants in a population, not just two. The two alleles are two inherited copies of the gene, not two different proteins.",
"L17-119": "The functional significance of a variation needs to be known to explain the patient's drug response phenotype. Functional significance is what a change in a gene does to how the gene's product works, whether that product is a protein, an enzyme or a transporter. The drug response comes from how that product works. In class she said that if we don't know how that genetic variation is changing how the gene product works, we cannot explain our drug response phenotype. The traps confuse function with other properties. Whether a variant is a polymorphism depends on frequency, not function. Which parent passed it on is inheritance, and where it sits on the chromosome is the locus.",
"L17-120": "Information about the star two allele of cytochrome P four fifty two C nineteen can guide whether a patient gets a drug or a lower dose because its functional significance is known. The genotype-phenotype relationship for star two is tight. The enzyme it makes has no activity, and even the reason it is not active is known. In class she said that we know the functional significance of these genetic variants, and that is why we can use the information to decide whether a patient should get a drug, or whether to reduce the dose. Frequency does not make a variant usable for dosing. Star two is a variant with no activity, not the wild type. This enzyme metabolizes drugs; it is not a receptor.",
"L17-121": "A drug response phenotype is often hard to predict from one gene because several genes can each add to the response. Most traits are multigenic rather than monogenic, and drug response can be multigenic too. A variant in one gene may have a large impact, while a variant in another gene has a smaller one. In class she said that a lot of the time our response to medications is affected by more than one genetic variation, and that this genotype and this genotype added together result in an adverse response. One gene can carry many polymorphisms, not just one site. Patients are genotyped, and for some drugs it is standard protocol. Frequency is not the complication, since many of these variants are polymorphisms.",
"L17-122": "Two patients with the same variant can respond differently because environment and epigenetic factors change how genes are expressed. Diet, habits, lack of exercise, sleep and stress affect gene expression. More internal factors, D N A methylation and histone modification, are called epigenetic factors. In class she said that other things affect how our genes get expressed and will affect the significance of a genetic variation, and so influence the genotype-phenotype relationship for drug response. Diet and stress do not change the D N A sequence; the two patients carry the same variant. Pharmacogenomics does not discuss variants as dominant or recessive. Frequency is a population measure, not a property of one person.",
"L17-123": "The three patterns appear more often than chance because the alleles are inherited together on one chromosome. A haplotype is a set of alleles at multiple loci that co-exist on the same chromosome, occurring at a frequency greater than random chance. That greater-than-chance co-occurrence is called linkage disequilibrium. Katzung defines it as the nonrandom association of alleles at two or more loci that descend from a single ancestral chromosome. In class she said there is something about the gene structure that makes those single nucleotide polymorphisms, or snips, happen in those groupings, and she added that the chemistry of D N A affects how likely genetic variations are to occur. The frequency of each single snip does not explain why they occur together. Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium is about frequencies staying constant across generations. A haplotype's loci share one chromosome, not different chromosomes.",
"L17-124": "A population can be described by genotype frequency as well as allele frequency because alleles make up each person's genotype. Allele frequency is how often a version of an allele occurs in a population. Genotype frequency is how often a genotype occurs. In class she said not to forget that your alleles make up your genotype, so people are described by a genotype frequency as well. The wild type allele frequency and the variant allele frequency together give the genotype frequency. Both alleles, not only the wild type, enter the genotype. Any allele, common or rare, has a frequency. Both measures describe a population and are usually given as a proportion or percentage, not measured within one person.",
"L17-125": "A study population must be in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium before its results are applied widely because equilibrium shows that no pressure has changed it from the general population. Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium means allele and genotype frequencies stay constant from generation to generation unless specific influences are introduced. In class she said that it is basically evolutionary pressure: you can't find that your population has changed compared to the general population, or you won't be in equilibrium. A sample out of equilibrium does not represent the general population, so its results cannot be generalized. Equilibrium says nothing about each subject carrying the variant or about the drug's efficacy. Haplotypes occurring more often than chance is linkage disequilibrium.",
});
